14:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1c",null,{"guide":{"date":"May 9–14, 1864","place":"Beaver Dam Station, Yellow Tavern, Richmond, and the Chickahominy River, Virginia","result":"Sheridan defeated Stuart’s smaller force at Yellow Tavern, mortally wounded the Confederate cavalry commander, fought through the Meadow Bridges, and reached the James River after damaging two railroads and Confederate supplies","facts":[{"label":"Sheridan’s starting force","value":"About 12,000","note":"Seven cavalry brigades, two horse-artillery brigades, and 32 guns"},{"label":"Length of the U.S. column","value":"About 13 miles"},{"label":"Stuart’s force at Yellow Tavern","value":"About 3,000"},{"label":"Locomotives destroyed at Beaver Dam","value":"2"},{"label":"Railcars burned","value":"About 100"},{"label":"Rations destroyed","value":"About 1.5 million"},{"label":"U.S. prisoners released","value":"300–400"},{"label":"Raid casualties","value":"About 1,200 total","note":"The episode estimates roughly 600 on each side"}],"maps":[{"title":"Troop positions at Yellow Tavern","caption":"The schematic shows Lomax blocking Telegraph Road, Wickham positioned north on the ridge, the second Confederate line across the road, and Custer’s mounted attack. It is based on the National Park Service battlefield account; positions are approximate.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/frsp/planyourvisit/yell-tav.htm","image":"/images/yellow-tavern-tactical-map.svg","alt":"Schematic tactical map of the Battle of Yellow Tavern on May 11, 1864"},{"title":"The raid from Spotsylvania toward Richmond","caption":"Sheridan left the Spotsylvania fighting, crossed the North and South Anna rivers, damaged the Virginia Central and Richmond, Fredericksburg and Potomac railroads, and approached Richmond from the north.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448882/","image":"/images/overland-campaign-1864-map.jpg","alt":"Historical map of the 1864 Virginia campaign between Spotsylvania and Richmond"},{"title":"Yellow Tavern and Richmond today","caption":"The battlefield was at the Telegraph and Mountain Road junction north of Richmond. Interstate 295 now cuts across the former Confederate position, and modern development covers most of the field.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-77.62%2C37.51%2C-77.28%2C37.82&layer=mapnik&marker=37.6770%2C-77.4587"}],"moments":[{"time":"1:23","audioTime":83,"title":"Grant’s supporting operations were ending","detail":"The episode follows the Confederate victory at New Market, which released about 2,500 troops for Lee. Butler and Sheridan still threatened Richmond from opposite directions."},{"time":"3:42","audioTime":222,"title":"Sheridan received permission to seek Stuart","detail":"After arguing with George Meade about cavalry performance, Sheridan said he could defeat J. E. B. Stuart. Grant supported the proposal and Meade issued the order."},{"time":"4:48","audioTime":288,"title":"The cavalry corps departed May 9","detail":"Sheridan’s force left the Spotsylvania area at daybreak in a thirteen-mile column of about 12,000 men and 32 guns."},{"time":"5:48","audioTime":348,"title":"Stuart kept four brigades with Lee’s army","detail":"He pursued with Fitzhugh Lee’s two brigades and later James B. Gordon’s brigade. The remaining cavalry stayed near Lee in case Grant moved around another flank."},{"time":"7:20","audioTime":440,"title":"Stuart tried to catch the column at the North Anna","detail":"He sent Wickham directly behind Sheridan while taking Gordon and Lomax upstream. Sheridan guarded the crossing and moved the full column safely south of the river."},{"time":"8:01","audioTime":481,"title":"U.S. cavalry destroyed the Beaver Dam depot","detail":"The raid destroyed two locomotives, about one hundred railcars, medical stores, and roughly 1.5 million rations, and released between three hundred and four hundred U.S. prisoners."},{"time":"8:35","audioTime":515,"title":"Stuart divided his pursuit","detail":"Gordon stayed behind Sheridan while Stuart and Fitzhugh Lee moved southeast to Telegraph Road, attempting to pass the U.S. column before it reached Richmond."},{"time":"9:30","audioTime":570,"title":"The Confederate march continued through exhaustion","detail":"Stuart’s troopers halted near Taylorsville after thirty-two hours in the saddle, rested six hours, and resumed the march at 3 a.m. on May 11."},{"time":"10:17","audioTime":617,"title":"Sheridan stopped to rest at Ground Squirrel Bridge","detail":"His objective was to fight Stuart rather than win a race to Richmond. The U.S. cavalry bivouacked south of the South Anna River on May 10."},{"time":"11:19","audioTime":679,"title":"Ten thousand U.S. cavalrymen resumed the advance","detail":"Sheridan left John I. Gregg’s 2,000-man brigade to guard the rear while the main force rode south on May 11."},{"time":"12:03","audioTime":723,"title":"Gordon attacked the U.S. rear guard","detail":"Confederate cavalry crossed at a nearby ford and began a running fight. Henry Davies’s U.S. brigade also damaged the Richmond, Fredericksburg and Potomac Railroad near Ashland."},{"time":"12:51","audioTime":771,"title":"The opposing columns converged on Yellow Tavern","detail":"Sheridan used Mountain Road while Stuart came down Telegraph Road. The roads met at an abandoned inn north of Richmond."},{"time":"13:26","audioTime":806,"title":"Lomax blocked the crossroads","detail":"His brigade reached Yellow Tavern around 8 a.m. and formed along Telegraph Road. Stuart had arrived ahead of the U.S. column."},{"time":"14:18","audioTime":858,"title":"Wickham formed a second wing on the ridge","detail":"Lomax faced west near the junction while Wickham stood about half a mile north, facing south above Turner’s Run and positioned to fire into an advancing U.S. flank."},{"time":"14:47","audioTime":887,"title":"Stuart had about 3,000 troops immediately available","detail":"The odds were worse than three to one. Stuart expected the Richmond garrison to join the battle and help close Sheridan’s route of withdrawal."},{"time":"15:53","audioTime":953,"title":"Merritt opened the battle","detail":"Wesley Merritt’s division dismounted, crossed a cornfield, and attacked with seven-shot Spencer carbines. The first assault captured about two hundred Confederates and drove Lomax north."},{"time":"16:54","audioTime":1014,"title":"U.S. scouts reached Richmond’s outer works","detail":"Two regiments found the works unmanned and heard alarm bells in the city. They saw no large force preparing to march against Sheridan."},{"time":"18:59","audioTime":1139,"title":"Stuart asked Richmond for an infantry attack","detail":"Braxton Bragg reported that 4,000 reservists and government clerks held the city while three veteran brigades came from Petersburg. Stuart requested that Eppa Hunton’s brigade strike Sheridan’s flank."},{"time":"20:09","audioTime":1209,"title":"Sheridan attacked again at 4 p.m.","detail":"Three U.S. divisions moved against Fitzhugh Lee’s two brigades during a thunderstorm. The Confederate infantry Stuart expected had not arrived."},{"time":"20:51","audioTime":1251,"title":"The Confederate line broke within thirty minutes","detail":"Most units fought dismounted, but mounted U.S. attacks penetrated the position. Fitzhugh Lee withdrew the survivors across the Chickahominy River."},{"time":"21:20","audioTime":1280,"title":"John A. Huff mortally wounded Stuart","detail":"The 5th Michigan Cavalry private shot Stuart in the abdomen while the general tried to rally the center of his line. Fitzhugh Lee assumed command."},{"time":"22:17","audioTime":1337,"title":"Stuart was taken to Richmond","detail":"An ambulance made a six-hour trip to the home of Stuart’s brother-in-law, Dr. Charles Brewer. Stuart died there at 7:38 p.m. on May 12 before his wife arrived."},{"time":"23:53","audioTime":1433,"title":"Sheridan tried to pass east of Richmond","detail":"Flooded water and the city defenses forced the U.S. cavalry toward the damaged Meadow Bridges over the Chickahominy River."},{"time":"24:51","audioTime":1491,"title":"Confederate forces converged at Meadow Bridges","detail":"Fitzhugh Lee held the far bank, Gordon attacked from the west, and Archibald Gracie’s infantry advanced from Richmond while U.S. troops crossed the railroad bridge and repaired the road bridges."},{"time":"26:03","audioTime":1563,"title":"The U.S. cavalry cleared the crossings","detail":"By about 4 p.m. on May 12 the Meadow Bridges were repaired and Confederate forces had withdrawn. Sheridan crossed and moved toward Haxall’s Landing on the James."},{"time":"26:21","audioTime":1581,"title":"Sheridan reached the James River","detail":"The cavalry encamped at Haxall’s Landing on May 14, contacted U.S. gunboats, and reported the raid’s results to Grant."},{"time":"26:52","audioTime":1612,"title":"The raid cost about 1,200 casualties","detail":"The episode estimates roughly six hundred casualties on each side. It also caused major supply losses, temporary railroad damage, and the death of Stuart."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"blue","branches":[{"commander":"Philip H. Sheridan","command":"Cavalry Corps, Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"David McM. Gregg, Wesley Merritt, and James H. Wilson’s divisions"},{"commander":"Wesley Merritt","command":"1st Cavalry Division","subordinates":"Led the first attack at Yellow Tavern; George A. Custer’s brigade made the decisive mounted charge"},{"commander":"David McM. Gregg","command":"2nd Cavalry Division","subordinates":"John I. Gregg’s brigade guarded the rear against Gordon"},{"commander":"James H. Wilson","command":"3rd Cavalry Division","subordinates":"Moved with Sheridan’s main column and joined the final attack"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"red","branches":[{"commander":"J. E. B. Stuart","command":"Cavalry Corps, Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Directed the pursuit and the Yellow Tavern defense until mortally wounded"},{"commander":"Fitzhugh Lee","command":"Cavalry division","subordinates":"William C. Wickham and Lunsford L. Lomax’s brigades; assumed command after Stuart fell"},{"commander":"James B. Gordon","command":"Cavalry brigade","subordinates":"Attacked Sheridan’s rear guard and later approached the Meadow Bridges"},{"commander":"Braxton Bragg","command":"Military adviser and Richmond defense coordinator","subordinates":"4,000 reservists and clerks in the city with veteran infantry arriving from Petersburg"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Philip H. Sheridan","role":"Led the 12,000-man raid and concentrated three divisions against Stuart at Yellow Tavern","side":"United States","image":"/images/philip-sheridan.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2017896949/"},{"name":"George A. Custer","role":"Led the mounted attack that penetrated the Confederate line during the final phase","side":"United States","image":"/images/sheridan-generals.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2009633605/"},{"name":"John A. Huff","role":"5th Michigan Cavalry private who fired the shot that mortally wounded Stuart","side":"United States"},{"name":"Wesley Merritt","role":"Commanded the lead division and drove Lomax from the first Confederate position","side":"United States","image":"/images/sheridan-generals.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2009633605/"},{"name":"J. E. B. Stuart","role":"Commanded the smaller Confederate force and was mortally wounded while rallying its center","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/jeb-stuart.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2014646250/"},{"name":"Fitzhugh Lee","role":"Commanded the two brigades at Yellow Tavern and took over after Stuart was wounded","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Lunsford L. Lomax","role":"Held the first position along Telegraph Road and then joined the second line","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"William C. Wickham","role":"Held the ridge north of the crossroads and fired into the U.S. flank","side":"Confederacy"}],"losses":[{"name":"J. E. B. Stuart","side":"Confederacy","role":"Cavalry Corps commander, Army of Northern Virginia","outcome":"Shot in the abdomen at Yellow Tavern on May 11 and died in Richmond at 7:38 p.m. on May 12"},{"name":"Henry Clay Pate","side":"Confederacy","role":"Colonel, 5th Virginia Cavalry","outcome":"Killed during the first phase while Lomax’s brigade defended the Telegraph Road position"}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/yellow-tavern-tactical-map.svg","alt":"Schematic of the two phases of the Battle of Yellow Tavern","caption":"The first U.S. attack forced Lomax back; the final attack broke the combined Confederate line and mortally wounded Stuart.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/frsp/planyourvisit/yell-tav.htm"},{"image":"/images/jeb-stuart.jpg","alt":"Civil War portrait of J. E. B. Stuart in uniform","caption":"Stuart reached Yellow Tavern ahead of Sheridan but had only about 3,000 exhausted cavalrymen immediately available.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2014646250/"},{"image":"/images/philip-sheridan.jpg","alt":"Portrait of Philip H. Sheridan","caption":"Sheridan used his numerical advantage to bring up three divisions for the 4 p.m. attack.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2017896949/"},{"image":"/images/jeb-stuart-grave.jpg","alt":"Temporary marker at J. E. B. Stuart’s grave in Hollywood Cemetery","caption":"Stuart died in Richmond on May 12 and was buried at Hollywood Cemetery.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666805/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast, episode 489","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2025/04/14/civil-war-podcast-episode-489/"},{"label":"National Park Service: Battle of Yellow Tavern","url":"https://www.nps.gov/frsp/planyourvisit/yell-tav.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Yellow Tavern battle detail","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=va052"},{"label":"Library of Congress: 1864 Virginia campaign map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448882/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: J. E. B. Stuart portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2014646250/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: J. E. B. Stuart’s temporary grave marker","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666805/"}]}}]}]
